WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.130
It's January as I film this, and Kyoto is in the coldest part of winter.

2
00:00:03.590 --> 00:00:06.150
And what we make in this kind of cold is miso.

3
00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:10.930
In Japanese we say "kanjikomi," which means making fermented foods in the cold.

4
00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:13.550
Why is the cold better?

5
00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:16.670
Green soybeans are very sweet.

6
00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:20.730
When you taste them after boiling,

7
00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:22.970
I think that sweetness and flavor will surprise you.

8
00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:27.270
Cooking them this soft is the key.

9
00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:30.470
Please try one.

10
00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:35.790
It's so delicious, and sweet. Just like this. It's incredibly good.

11
00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:40.890
On the day you make miso, try eating it as a side dish for dinner.

12
00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:44.330
The most fun part of making miso

13
00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:48.050
may well be this step of breaking up the koji.

14
00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:49.210
And then there's the aroma.

15
00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:53.610
It has a soft, fluffy scent, a little like chestnuts.

16
00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:58.330
Koji gives off a light, lovely fragrance.

17
00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:02.810
I think making delicious miso that way is the best.

18
00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:10.640
And just like that, the miso balls are all done.

19
00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:14.840
Look how many we made. Now let's move on to storage.

20
00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:20.120
making it in the very coldest weather, miso really does turn out the best.

21
00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:22.840
Fermentation starts up very slowly,

